      "textEn": "492\n\nin. At night, when we sleep, all our actions come to a standstill, but as soon as we wake up,\nour memory immediately returns, and we start thinking, \"Oh, am I saying this?\" WhatdoIdo?\"\nThis is called the Sapnottitanyaya. Let's assume we die. \"To die\" means to remain inactive for some time\nand then resume one's work. This cycle continues according to our actions and nature,\nthrough many lifetimes. If in human life, we start our activities with the work of spiritual life,\nthen we return to real life and attain accomplishment. Otherwise, according to karma,\nnature, etc., various kinds of life and actions keep going on, and in the same way, our\nbirth and death also keep going on. Bhakti Vinod Thakur has said—Maya's control or whatever, Khasshe\nHabudub Bhai—Dear brothers! Why are you drifting away in the waves of illusion? Only upon attaining spiritual status\ncan actions become permanent.\nKrutapunyapunjaha—Only when many meritorious deeds of life are accumulated,\ndoes this state of being attained. Janmakotisukrutaina labhyate (Cātanaṃcāravāṭaṃ mūṇaṃ cātūraṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitasikaṃ caitas The Krishna consciousness movement wants to stop the cycle of birth and death, Kotijanma. In one birth,\nto live a stable life by fixing everything is Krishna's name. The twenty-four hours are seen from the horizon. Vrindavanam janajivyadrumakinkaranam samapriyam. 59. Meaning of the word:\n\nSapadi—immediately; Eva—indeed; Abhitah—all around; Pashyant—seeing; Dishah—in the directions; Apashyat—Brahma saw;\nPurah-sthitam—in front; Vrindavanam—Vrindavan; Jan-ajivya-druma-akirnam—filled with trees, which were the means of livelihood for the residents; Sam-apriyam—and which were equally pleasant in all seasons..\n\nThen, upon looking in all directions, Lord Brahma immediately saw Vrindavan before him, which was\nfilled with trees, which were the means of livelihood for the inhabitants, and were equally\npleasing in all seasons.\nMeaning: Janajeevadrumakirnam—Trees and plants are essential and they are pleasant in all seasons. It is the same case in Vrindavan. It is not that trees look pleasant in one season\nand do not look in another season, rather they are going to look pleasant in all the seasonal changes. Trees and\nplants can be the means of real livelihood for everyone. Sarvakamdugama (Bhagavata)"
