      "textEn": "493\n\n1..4). Trees and vegetation provide the real means of life, not industry. Where there are no natural enemies, there are deer and other animals. Mitranivajitavasadrutarutshakadikam. 60. Meaning of the word\n\nWhere—where; natural—by nature; Durvairah—being enemies; Saha-asan—living together; Nru—human; Mrig-adayah—and animals;\nMitran—friends; Iva—like; Ajit—of Shri Krishna; Aasan—abode; Drut—having gone; Rut—anger; Tarpak-\nAdikam—thirst, etc..\n\nVrindavan is the divine abode of Lord Krishna, where there is neither hunger, nor anger, nor thirst. Although there is natural animosity between humans and\nviolent animals, they live together here with divine friendship. Meaning: forest means jungle. We do not want to go there fearing the jungle, but the wild animals of Vrindavan are like gods because they do not hold animosity. Even in this physical world, in the jungle,\nanimals live together, and when they go to drink water, they do not attack anyone.\nThe reason for the generation of animosity\nis sensual indulgence, but there is no sensual indulgence in Vrindavan because there, the satisfaction of Krishna is the only goal of everyone. Even in this physical world, the animals of Vrindavan do not harbor animosity towards the monks living in the forest. The monks take care of the cows and feed the lions milk, and they tell them to come here\nand drink a little milk. In this way, there is no trace of animosity and jealousy in Vrindavan. This is the only difference between Vrindavan and the general world. We get scared hearing the name of the forest, but in Vrindavan,\nthere is no such fear. There, each person is happy by pleasing Krishna. Krishnothkirtanaganartanaparaun. Whether\nhe is Goswami, or a lion, or any other ferocious animal—each one's task is the same—to please\nKrishna. The lion is also a devotee. This is the specialty of Vrindavan. Every person in Vrindavan is happy. There, the calf\nis happy, the cat and the dog are happy, and the human is also happy.\nEveryone, according to their ability,\nwants to serve Krishna, therefore there is no trace of animosity. Someone sometimes thinks\nthat the monkeys of Vrindavan are enemies because they snatch food and create chaos, but\nwe see that monkeys are also allowed to eat butter there, as Lord Krishna himself does. Krishna\nhas shown himself that every creature has the right to live. This is the life of Vrindavan. Why would I stay alive when you'd rather I die? No. This is material life. Resident of Vrindavan"
